content:
You can left-align a table caption in LaTeX without the `caption` package by redefining `\@makecaption` or using simpler inline tricks. Here are a few approaches:

## Option 1: Redefine `\@makecaption` (most robust)

```latex
\documentclass{article}

\makeatletter
\long\def\@makecaption#1#2{%
  \vskip\abovecaptionskip
  \sbox\@tempboxa{#1: #2}%
  \ifdim \wd\@tempboxa >\hsize
    #1: #2\par
  \else
    \noindent #1: #2\par   % <- left-aligned instead of \centering
  \fi
  \vskip\belowcaptionskip}
\makeatother

\begin{document}
\begin{table}
\caption{My left-aligned caption}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
A & B \\
C & D
\end{tabular}
\end{table}
\end{document}
```

## Option 2: Quick inline trick

Wrap the table contents in a `flushleft` environment:

```latex
\begin{table}
\begin{flushleft}
\caption{My left-aligned caption}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
A & B \\
C & D
\end{tabular}
\end{flushleft}
\end{table}
```

## Option 3: Use `\raggedright` inside a group

```latex
\begin{table}
{\raggedright
\caption{My left-aligned caption}\par}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
A & B \\
C & D
\end{tabular}
\end{table}
```

Option 1 is the cleanest for document-wide consistency; Option 2 is the simplest for one-off use.

